+## Existing source route
+
+1. Ordinary input.primary carries an observed grip pose through the existing
+   Pico OpenXR Session. The real controller/pointer path produces Entity
+   pointer events; WebEntityRenderer::handlePointerEvent maps their hit point
+   to an actual mouse event on the owning offscreen QQuickWindow
+   (RenderableWebEntityItem.cpp:620–668). NativeWebViewItem::mousePressEvent
+   forceActiveFocuses that original item and sends the actual pointer to Java
+   (:636). Actual current-focus/DOM originals must establish editor focus;
+   neither a Session ACK nor a requested control supplies it.
+   The controller-specific Entity focus join is explicitly wired in
+   Application_Setup.cpp:1976–1989: triggerBeginEntity invokes the original
+   keyboardFocusOperator, tests event.shouldFocus()/wantsKeyboardFocus, and
+   sets that real Entity as the keyboard focus. Keyboard key entities are
+   deliberately prevented from stealing that established Entity focus.
+2. World QML selects +android_picoInterface/Web3DSurface.qml, then the shared
+   Android World surface, EmbeddedWebView and texture EmbeddedWebBackend. Source
+   identity comes from the original World context. Editor focus raises the
+   actual keyboard; the old finished document and current grant are separate.
+3. Ordinary VR keys come from Keyboard's configured key strings. Accepted
+   trigger -> handleTriggerBegin -> dispatchKey ->
+   Application::dispatchVirtualKeyboardEvent -> actual focused Entity event
+   handler -> QQuickWindow -> NativeWebViewItem::keyPressEvent. The unchanged
+   stock layout has 124 ASCII keys/three layers. It has no Unicode composition
+   producer. PASTE emits Ctrl+V, which NativeWebViewItem rejects with other
+   Ctrl/Alt/Meta keys before editorCommand. No clipboard route is qualified.
+4. A different, existing production Qt input route accepts Unicode IME:
